# Why do in-between moves wreck your calculation?
teaches: ["calculation/in-between-move","tactics/desperado"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What is an in-between move (zwischenzug)? What is a desperado, and why play it?"
next: {"title":"How do two tactics combine?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/13-how-do-two-tactics-combine.cast"}

## Warm-up: a mate and a fork
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: rn1qkbnr/ppp2p1p/3p2p1/4N3/2B1P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BbK2R w KQkq - 0 6, moves: [Bxf7+, Ke7, Nd5#]}
      - {fen: r1b1k2r/pq3ppp/1p2p3/2p5/4N3/2P5/PP2QPPP/3R1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nd6+, Kf8, Nxb7, Bxb7]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: why in-between moves wreck your calculation. First, two warm-ups from last week: a mate and a fork.
    draw position

B: Black's bishop just took my queen. I take it back with the king.
    highlight piece_d1

A: Before you take back, the Three Questions. Any checks?
    highlight piece_c4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line true hints ["A check that takes a pawn.","Then the knight on c3."] themes ["mating pattern"] voice a teaches ["tactics/mating-patterns"]
        right: Bishop takes f7, check; the king must go to e7, and knight to d5 is mate.
        wrong: Start with a check. Which of your pieces can give one?

B: Mate with three little pieces, and his bishop is still sitting on my queen's square.
    point at.ref piece_d1 gesture circle

A: That's Légal's mate, named after Légal, a French master of the 1700s.
    highlight piece_f7 piece_d5 piece_e5

A: Second one: a fork.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: His king and queen are both on light squares. That's the knight's scanner.
    highlight piece_e8 piece_b7

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A knight check.","Which square touches the king and the queen?"] themes ["fork"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork"]
        right: Knight to d6, check: it hits the king and the queen. The king steps aside, and the queen falls for a knight.
        wrong: Look for a knight move that gives check and hits the queen too.

B: Check first, so the queen had no time to run.
    point at.ref sq_d6 gesture circle

A: Both started with a forcing move. Today: the forcing move nobody expects, slipped in between.
    animate position_shown 0 plies_shown 3 duration 0.6

## The move in between
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1bqkb1r/pppn1ppp/5n2/3p2B1/3P4/2N5/PP2PPPP/R2QKBNR w KQkq - 0 6, moves: [Nxd5, Nxd5, Bxd8], arrows: [{from: g5, to: d8}]}
      - {fen: r1bBkb1r/pppn1ppp/8/3n4/3P4/8/PP2PPPP/R2QKBNR b KQkq - 0 7, moves: [Bb4+, Qd2, Bxd2+, Kxd2, Kxd8], flip: true}
      - {fen: r4rk1/p1p1qppp/1pnpn3/8/3PPB2/2N3P1/PP3P1P/R2Q1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [d5, Nxf4, gxf4, Nb4], arrows: [{from: d5, to: c6}, {from: d5, to: e6}]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: elephant
      title: Queen's Gambit Declined, Elephant Trap
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      finding: After 6.Nxd5? Nxd5 7.Bxd8 Bb4+ White's only legal move is 8.Qd2, and after Bxd2+ Kxd2 Kxd8 Black is a piece up.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Queen%27s_Gambit_Declined,_Elephant_Trap

    card title "The move in between"

A: Now the lesson: the Elephant Trap, which you met on day five. This time, see why it works. White to move.
    draw position

B: The knight on f6 is pinned to the queen. So I take the pawn on d5, for free.
    highlight piece_g5 piece_f6 piece_d8

A: Play it.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

A: The pinned knight takes back anyway.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

B: Then I take his queen! A queen for a knight.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: That's where your count stopped. Now sit in Black's chair: the board turns round.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Before you take back." widget chess line 1 hints ["Checks first.","The bishop on f8."] themes ["in-between move"] voice a
        right: Bishop to b4, check. The queen on d8 can wait: it isn't going anywhere.
        wrong: Before you take back on d8, look for a check.

A: White has only one legal move: block with the queen.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: The bishop takes it, with check,
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: the king takes back,
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.3

A: and only now does Black take back on d8.
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 1.3

A: White took a pawn, the queen and a bishop. Black took a knight, the queen and a bishop: a knight for a pawn.
    highlight piece_d5

A: Bishop to b4 was an in-between move: zwischenzug, in German.
    point at.ref sq_b4 gesture circle

A: A forcing move slipped in where everyone expected the recapture.
    highlight piece_d8

B: So the pin wasn't real?
    point at.ref piece_d5 gesture circle

A: Back to the start, on White's side.
    animate position_shown 0 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

A: The pin was real: the knight on f6 stood in front of the queen.
    draw arrow_0

A: But your count assumed Black takes back at once.
    highlight piece_f6

    erase arrow_0

A: In-between moves have a cousin: the desperado.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

B: Pawn to d5!
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.3

B: It forks both knights. One of them has to fall.
    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

A: The knight on e6 is lost anyway.
    highlight piece_e6

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1

A: So it takes something on the way out.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

A: You take back,
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: and the other knight walks away. Bishop for knight: your fork won nothing.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.3

A: A desperado is a doomed piece that sells itself as dearly as it can.
    point at.ref sq_f4 gesture circle

A: Both break one assumption: that the other side takes back. At every capture in your head, ask the Three Questions for the side to move.
    highlight piece_f4 piece_b4

## Your turn
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 6k1/pb1r1ppp/1p6/8/8/1qN5/PPP2PPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Re8#]}
      - {fen: r2Qr3/ppp2ppk/7p/5b2/8/R1N4P/PPP2PP1/4R1K1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Rxe1+, Kh2, Rxd8], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/3p1n2/4p3/4P3/2bP1N2/PPP1QPPP/R1B2RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [bxc3]}
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/pbp2ppp/1p3n2/3p4/2B1N3/4P3/PPP2PPP/R2Q1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nxf6+, Qxf6, Bxd5]}
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/pbpp1ppp/1p1n1n2/4P3/2B5/1QN5/PPP2PPP/R1B2RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Nxc4, Qxc4, d5, Qf4, Ne4], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you know both tricks: your turn. Five puzzles, scored.
    draw position

A: First one: Black has just taken your queen.
    highlight piece_b3

B: Pawn takes back. Queen for queen.
    highlight piece_a2

    pause 0.3

    ask "Before you take back." widget chess line true themes ["in-between move","back rank"] voice a test d12 teaches ["calculation/in-between-move","safety/back-rank"]
        right: Rook to e8 is mate: nothing guards Black's back rank, and the queen on b3 never mattered.
        wrong: Before the recapture, the Three Questions: any checks?

B: Mate. And his queen is still sitting on b3.
    point at.ref piece_b3 gesture circle

A: Next, you're Black. White has just taken your queen.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Before you take back." widget chess line true themes ["in-between move"] voice a test d12
        right: Rook takes e1, check: White's queen guarded that rook until she left d1. The king steps to h2, and you take back on d8: a rook up.
        wrong: Before you take back on d8, look for a capture with check.

A: Back to White. Black has just taken your knight on c3.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

B: So what's in between this time?
    highlight piece_c3

    pause 0.3

    ask "What do you play?" widget chess line true themes ["in-between move"] voice a test d12
        right: Pawn takes c3. No check or capture does better here: when the Three Questions find nothing, the recapture is the move.
        wrong: Run the Three Questions. If nothing forcing works, take back.

A: Next, a pawn forks your knight and bishop.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Save what you can." widget chess line 2 themes ["desperado"] voice a test d12
        right: Knight takes f6, check, and the queen takes back: knight for knight.
        wrong: One of the two is lost anyway. What can it take, with check?

A: Then your bishop takes the forking pawn: attacked twice, guarded once.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: Last one, and you're Black: both your knights are forked.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Save what you can." widget chess line 2 themes ["desperado"] voice a test d12
        right: Knight takes c4, and the queen takes back: your doomed knight took a bishop with it.
        wrong: One knight is lost anyway. What is the most it can take?

A: Now pawn to d5 hits the queen first: another in-between move.
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

A: The queen steps away,
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.3

A: and your other knight gets out.
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 1.3

    erase position

    text score_big "Day 12 score" x 500 y 400 font_size 96

A: You scored {d12.score} of {d12.total}.
    results test d12

    erase score_big

    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 2 duration 0.3

B: The desperados felt like giving a piece away.
    draw position

A: It was gone already. You only chose what it took with it.
    highlight piece_c4

    erase position

A: So a trade isn't over when you've counted it.
    text syn_capture "every capture" x 500 y 470 font_size 72

A: At every capture, the side to move may not take back.
    text syn_q "who's to move?" x 500 y 360 font_size 72

    erase syn_capture syn_q

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    text hw_game "one slow game: 15 + 10" x 500 y 440 font_size 56
    text hw_mistake "first mistake?" x 500 y 350 font_size 56

## Where next
end_page: true

